   "source": [
    "## Identification and Estimation\n",
    "\n",
    "### What the Designed Variation Buys"
   ],
   "id": "dcc1cbe6-d245-4bc1-9950-42b7fbf64ef3"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "raw_mimetype": "text/html"
   },
   "source": [
    "<!--\n",
    "TODO: prose. The substance, from the papers now read in full:\n",
    "\n",
    "(a) POSITION ENTERS SEARCH COSTS, NOT UTILITY. @Ursu_2018 §4.3 enumerates all seven\n",
    "ways position could act (on v, on epsilon, on c, and combinations) and rules out all\n",
    "but c using three tests:\n",
    "   - purchases conditional on click are unaffected by position -> rules out any case\n",
    "     where position enters pre-search utility v (her Cases 1, 4, 5, 7);\n",
    "   - consumers click TOP-ranked alternatives first -> rules out position acting on\n",
    "     the post-search variance, which would predict reservation utility INCREASING in\n",
    "     position (her Cases 2 and, under common parameter values, 6);\n",
    "   - an exogenous demotion (opaque offers) lowers clicks on the demoted hotel ->\n",
    "     rules IN position acting on search costs.\n",
    "We can run the first two natively and better: we randomize position in every task and\n",
    "we observe click order directly, which she had to import from a companion data set.\n",
    "The third we do not need, because our demotions ARE the design.\n",
    "This is the argument that licenses putting position in Z and not in X. It should be\n",
    "run and reported as a design check in every SIFT study, not assumed.\n",
    "\n",
    "(b) SEPARATING PREFERENCES FROM SEARCH COSTS. @Morozov_2021 §4.3.2: stopping decisions\n",
    "depend on the REALIZED utilities of already-searched alternatives but not on their\n",
    "search costs, which are sunk. So trade xi against c holding the mean reservation value\n",
    "z-bar fixed: search order is unchanged, but the higher intercept raises realized utility\n",
    "and makes stopping more likely. Their Appendix D sharpens this into an observable\n",
    "moment -- the RECALL RATE, the probability of purchasing an alternative searched\n",
    "earlier in the sequence, responds to the intercept but NOT to search cost. We observe\n",
    "recall directly (free recall, full click sequence), which makes this our cleanest\n",
    "separation moment.\n",
    "\n",
    "(c) WHAT THE PANEL BUYS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. @Morozov_2021 §4.3 proves nonparametric\n",
    "identification of individual-level preferences AND search costs from panel search data,\n",
    "but the argument assumes the number of sessions per consumer goes to infinity; with\n",
    "2-35 sessions (mean 5) they estimate hyperparameters only, not individual parameters\n",
    "(their §5.1). At T = 10 we should claim hierarchical posteriors in the conjoint\n",
    "tradition, NOT nonparametric individual identification. Say this plainly.\n",
    "\n",
    "(d) WHAT NEITHER PANEL NOR SHIFTER BUYS ALONE. @Morozov_2021 has the panel and still\n",
    "normalizes sigma_epsilon, reporting that little variation in their data speaks to it\n",
    "(their Appendix C); @Yavorsky_2021 has an exogenous shifter and no panel, and estimates\n",
    "it. SIFT has both, and -- see @sec-model-setup -- part of the post-search variance is\n",
    "fixed by the design rather than assumed. This is the sharpest version of the\n",
    "identification claim and belongs in the introduction.\n",
    "\n",
    "(e) SEARCH-COST LEVELS AND SPELL LENGTH. @Ursu_2018 §6.1 reports baseline search costs\n",
    "above $102 and attributes the magnitude to observing one click per search impression\n",
    "with no way to link a consumer's impressions; restricting to impressions with two or\n",
    "more clicks cuts mean search costs by up to 83%. Single-spell data mechanically\n",
    "inflates search costs. The panel is a levels argument, not only a heterogeneity\n",
    "argument.\n",
    "-->"
   ],
   "id": "7551fd46-a0e3-4128-8550-894837dd22f5"
  },
