   "source": [
    "### Testing the Zero-Search-Cost Restriction"
   ],
   "id": "b9fffd77-a962-4334-93f6-df19c9208791"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "raw_mimetype": "text/html"
   },
   "source": [
    "<!--\n",
    "TODO. IMPORTANT: the test cannot be a Wald test on the estimated search cost.\n",
    "Zero is a limit rather than an interior point -- c -> 0 corresponds to g(c) -> +infinity\n",
    "and log c -> -infinity -- so the null sits at a boundary in every parameterization.\n",
    "Construct the test from observable implications instead:\n",
    "   - holdout fit of SIFT against the nested conjoint model;\n",
    "   - the observable implication itself (does anyone leave alternatives unopened);\n",
    "   - a likelihood-ratio comparison with a simulated rather than chi-squared reference\n",
    "     distribution.\n",
    "Note also that this argues for estimating c rather than g directly, even though\n",
    "estimating g avoids the root-find during optimization (@Ursu_Seiler_Honka_2024 §5.2).\n",
    "-->"
   ],
   "id": "a5cf5fdb-ae6d-4489-a00c-8ed7b6c50b78"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Individual-Level Estimation from a Task Panel"
   ],
   "id": "072d5353-82a9-4bb0-b1f3-004abb3ede1f"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "raw_mimetype": "text/html"
   },
   "source": [
    "<!--\n",
    "TODO. Two routes to the individual-level deliverable:\n",
    "   1. Full hierarchical Bayes. What the conjoint audience expects, and what makes\n",
    "      individual part-worths and individual search costs directly reportable.\n",
    "   2. Two-stage empirical Bayes: estimate the hyperparameters by simulated maximum\n",
    "      likelihood with importance sampling, then draw individual posteriors by\n",
    "      Metropolis-Hastings conditional on the estimated hyperparameters. This is\n",
    "      exactly what @Morozov_2021 do to compute personalized prices (their §7.2 and\n",
    "      Appendix H), and it is far cheaper than full HB.\n",
    "Route 2 is the fallback if route 1 will not run at commercial sample sizes.\n",
    "-->"
   ],
   "id": "a116f73b-372c-4954-93af-3ceea70c0436"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Estimation"
   ],
   "id": "20138b2d-ed67-4781-9ab9-cedd164fac7a"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "raw_mimetype": "text/html"
   },
   "source": [
    "<!--\n",
    "TODO. Runtime is a binding practical constraint: a fit has to complete overnight, not\n",
    "over a week. The four candidate simulators, with published timings:\n",
    "\n",
    "  @Ursu_Seiler_Honka_2024 Table 2 (1,000 consumers, 5 sessions, 5 alternatives,\n",
    "  heterogeneous):\n",
    "    importance sampling    5h20m,  RMSE 0.01\n",
    "    kernel-smoothed       29h48m,  RMSE 0.13  (+ ~32h to calibrate the scaling vector)\n",
    "    GHK                  198h24m,  RMSE 0.09\n",
    "\n",
    "  @Chung_2025 Table 1 (1,000 consumers, 5 alternatives + outside option,\n",
    "  cross-sectional, 100 replications):\n",
    "    their GHK-type simulator   0.48 min,  SSE 0.040   <- one to two orders faster\n",
    "    kernel-smoothed            5-7 min,   SSE 0.21-2.98\n",
    "    crude frequency           15-127 min, SSE ~0.11\n",
    "    @Jiang_2021 pre-search-shock GHK  1.2-1.5 min, but needs a grid search over sigma_e\n",
    "\n",
    "LEADING CANDIDATE: @Chung_2025. Three separate mechanisms make it fast, and all three\n",
    "bite harder in our setting than in theirs:\n",
    "\n",
    "  1. NO TUNING CONSTANTS. It computes actual probabilities rather than kernel-smoothed\n",
    "     indicator violations, so there is no scaling vector to calibrate. That removes an\n",
    "     entire pre-estimation stage -- @Ursu_Seiler_Honka_2024 Appendix B report ~32 hours\n",
    "     for it -- and @Chung_2025 §6.3 show the stage cannot be shortcut, because on field\n",
    "     data the maximized likelihood declines mechanically in the scaling factor, so\n",
    "     calibration has to run through out-of-sample prediction instead.\n",
    "  2. NO ROOT-FINDING IN THE INNER LOOP. Bounds on reservation values are obtained by\n",
    "     evaluating g FORWARD on a utility difference -- closed form, vectorizable -- and\n",
    "     g-inverse is needed only for the K search costs actually drawn, from a spline\n",
    "     precomputed once before estimation (their footnote 7). With K << J, which is the\n",
    "     SIFT regime, this is most of the work removed. Contrast the reference R code,\n",
    "     which root-finds per alternative per respondent per likelihood evaluation.\n",
    "  3. IT SCALES WELL IN J WHEN K IS SMALL. Unsearched alternatives enter as a product of\n",
    "     closed-form cdf evaluations, one per alternative. This is the opposite of the\n",
    "     kernel simulator's behaviour, where a large J-K adds smoothed penalty terms that\n",
    "     come to dominate the objective (the smoothing-convention issue discussed above).\n",
    "\n",
    "WHAT ADOPTING IT COMMITS US TO. Their simulator requires the stochastic component of the\n",
    "reservation value to be the search cost rather than a pre-search taste shock\n",
    "(@sec-model-setup). Consequences to verify in @sec-simstudy rather than assume:\n",
    "  - sigma_epsilon. @Chung_2025 fix it at one, as everyone does. Our randomized position\n",
    "    enters as the location parameter of the search-cost distribution -- exactly the\n",
    "    shifted-exponential specification of their §6.2 -- so @Yavorsky_2021's shifter\n",
    "    argument should apply, but nobody has estimated sigma_epsilon inside this simulator.\n",
    "    This is the single most important thing the simulation study has to establish,\n",
    "    because @sec-managerial depends on it.\n",
    "  - Misspecification. Their Table 7 shows RATIOS of preference parameters are recovered\n",
    "    correctly even when the randomness is put in the wrong place; their Table 6 shows\n",
    "    LEVELS are not, and their search-cost levels swing wildly. Relative part-worths are\n",
    "    safe; monetized search costs are not. Say so.\n",
    "  - Estimate both specifications on the same simulated data and report the comparison.\n",
    "\n",
    "THE ONE PIECE THAT ALREADY EXISTS FOR US. @Chung_2025 Appendix B.4 works out the case\n",
    "where the outside option's utility is known prior to search, so respondents who click\n",
    "nothing are representable in the likelihood, and shows parameter recovery for it. That\n",
    "is SIFT's no-click exit, and it is the only treatment of it in the four reference\n",
    "implementations.\n",
    "\n",
    "CONFLICTING EVIDENCE ON IMPORTANCE SAMPLING, to resolve before relying on either:\n",
    "@Morozov_2021 Appendix G and @Ursu_Seiler_Honka_2024 Table 2 both find importance\n",
    "sampling fast and accurate; @Chung_2025 Table 3 find it badly biased -- heterogeneity\n",
    "standard deviations collapse and preference means shrink most of the way to zero. The\n",
    "likely explanation is resourcing rather than a defect: @Chung_2025 give it 100^2 draws\n",
    "per observation where @Morozov_2021 use up to 10^6, and importance sampling is acutely\n",
    "sensitive to the proposal density, which @Morozov_2021 centre on moment-matched starting\n",
    "values and @Chung_2025 do not describe. Worth settling in the simulation study, since\n",
    "importance sampling remains the fallback.\n",
    "\n",
    "PANEL AND HETEROGENEITY. The per-task likelihood contributions multiply across tasks with\n",
    "no structural change. For individual-level output, @Chung_2025 Appendix B.5 run their\n",
    "simulator inside a Metropolis-within-Gibbs sampler and compare against the Bayesian\n",
    "approach of @Morozov_2023, which they find biased and no faster.\n",
    "\n",
    "ORDER OF WORK: aggregate first (it validates against the reference implementations), then\n",
    "heterogeneity, then HB. See @sec-appendix for the validation protocol.\n",
    "\n",
    "STARTING VALUES. The effective-value representation of @Armstrong_2017 and @Choi_2018,\n",
    "w = min(u, z), gives purchase probabilities in near-probit form\n",
    "(@Ursu_Seiler_Honka_2024 §3.1). Too coarse to estimate from, but cheap enough to\n",
    "generate starting values, which buys optimizer iterations.\n",
    "-->"
   ],
   "id": "64346e06-df9b-46bb-9e3e-b66a2b32a1e3"
